---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const title = 'Methodology — Rate My Gov';
const description =
  'How Rate My Gov results are produced: voluntary anonymous responses, duplicate prevention, approximate geolocation, caching, and how to read the map and trends.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} canonical="https://ratemygov.in/methodology">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>

    <h2>What the results are</h2>
    <p>
      Rate My Gov collects voluntary, anonymous responses to a single question:
      whether people are satisfied or dissatisfied with governance in their state
      or Union Territory. The results reflect the people who chose to respond
      online. They are <strong>not a scientific or representative survey</strong> of
      the state's population.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rate My Gov is civic feedback. It is not affiliated with any political
      party, government, or candidate, and it does not endorse or oppose any of
      them.
    </p>

    <h2>How votes are recorded</h2>
    <p>
      Each person may record at most one vote per state or Union Territory per
      device and one per network (IP address). Duplicate prevention relies on a
      signed device cookie, a keyed hash of the device and network identity, and
      automated bot checks. Repeated attempts to vote more than once are not
      counted and never change the results.
    </p>

    <h2>Location</h2>
    <p>
      A voter's approximate country and state are inferred from the network
      connection (the IP address). Location is approximate and is used only to
      flag which state a response came from. It is <strong>not</strong> used to verify
      where a voter lives, and it is not proof of residency or citizenship.
    </p>

    <h2>How results update</h2>
    <p>
      Results pages are cached at the edge of the network and refresh about every
      30 seconds, so they load quickly and do not query a database on every
      visit. Votes are submitted through a separate request that is never cached.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the map</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Blue</strong> — a satisfied majority (more than half of the
        responses were satisfied).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Red</strong> — a dissatisfied majority (more than half of the
        responses were dissatisfied).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Neutral</strong> — an even split, treated in a neutral shade at
        exactly 50% satisfied and 50% dissatisfied.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Grey hatching</strong> — too few responses for a reliable
        reading (below the minimum threshold). A hatched state is
        <em>not</em> shown as 0% satisfied; it is simply not reported.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Colour is never the only way to read the map. Every region is a link to its
      own results table with exact percentages and response counts, and a full
      text table accompanies the map.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the trends</h2>
    <p>
      Trend views compare <strong>new votes recorded within a selected window</strong>
      (the last 7 or 30 days) with all-time results. A region with too few new
      votes in the window is shown as grey rather than reported as a percentage.
      Trends describe what respondents said over time; they do not imply a
      cause-and-effect relationship with any event or policy.
    </p>

    <h2>What these results mean</h2>
    <p>
      These results reflect voluntary online responses and are not representative
      of the state's population. They are civic feedback, not an election
      forecast and not an endorsement.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>